/* Deep original SC-300 instructional lessons, authored 2026-11-26. Educational simulation, not Microsoft reproduction. */
const V8_LESSONS=[{"id":"tenant-boundary","domain":"User identities","title":"Tenants, roles, and delegated administration","group":"Configure and manage a Microsoft Entra tenant","minutes":35,"skillHint":"built-in and custom roles, administrative units, effective permissions","objective":"Explain what a tenant protects, assign a narrow administrative role, and explain why administrative units do not create separate tenants.","analogy":"A tenant is the whole office building. A directory role gives a staff member an administrative job, while an administrative unit can limit which floors that job covers.","beginner":"Microsoft Entra stores identities and controls who can administer them. A user can have an ordinary work identity, an application entitlement, and a directory administration role—three different things. The last one is powerful because it changes other people's access. An administrative unit is a management scope, not a replacement for the company’s tenant or a network firewall.","technical":"Entra RBAC determines permitted directory actions from the assigned role and its supported scope. Tenant-wide roles can modify objects across the directory; administrative-unit-scoped assignments can limit supported management actions to objects included in that unit. Some permissions and services are tenant-wide and are not constrained by an AU. Review exact effective permissions and the least-privileged built-in role before considering a custom role. An AU administrator’s license requirements and available scoped roles vary by feature.","visual":[["Tenant","Identity security boundary for the fictional organization"],["User","Human or workload identity attempting an operation"],["Directory role","Named bundle of supported administrator permissions"],["Administrative unit","Optional object scope for a supported role assignment"],["Audit","Evidence of who changed role assignments and their scope"]],"caseStudy":"Houston helpdesk must reset passwords for employees in the Houston division but cannot manage global administrators. The security lead wants a scope design and documented emergency recovery.","steps":[["Open Roles & administrators","portal/roles","Compare Helpdesk Administrator with Global Administrator and Security Reader.","A role grants only its documented operations; viewing settings and modifying settings are different privileges."],["Create Houston Support administrative unit","portal/units","Create a fictional administrative unit named Houston Support.","An AU groups managed directory objects for supported scoped delegation."],["Inspect user accounts","portal/users","Choose a normal employee rather than the emergency account.","Scope only the users the support team is responsible for."],["Record a least-privilege role assignment","portal/roles","Select a supported helpdesk-oriented role with the narrowest available scope.","This simulator simplifies AU membership and cannot demonstrate every real permission boundary."]],"symptom":"A regional administrator can manage users outside their department.","reason":"The role was granted tenant-wide instead of at a supported AU scope, or the activity is not AU-scopable.","recover":"Check role-assignment scope, object membership, and the precise supported operations; remove broad grants and retest with a nonprivileged training identity.","q":"What is the best design for support staff who should administer only one region's users?","choices":["A supported directory role scoped to an administrative unit","Global Administrator for the whole tenant","Adding the staff to an app access group alone","Making every managed user a Guest"],"answer":0,"feedback":"Administrative units can scope supported role assignments. Application group membership alone is not an administrator role.","evidence":"Explain which permissions AU scope constrains and which remain tenant-wide. Provide a fictional role-assignment table and a rollback plan.","lab":"design","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/role-based-access-control/administrative-units"},{"id":"user-lifecycle","domain":"User identities","title":"Create, license, disable, and offboard an employee","group":"Create, configure, and manage Microsoft Entra identities","minutes":40,"skillHint":"users, licenses, bulk operations, revocation","objective":"Create a member identity with a unique UPN, assign an appropriate license, distinguish disabling accounts from revoking sessions, and offboard safely.","analogy":"Hiring creates an employee record; licensing turns on workplace services; leaving the company requires collecting access badges and disabling building access, not just removing a name from a team list.","beginner":"The user object is a record containing a display name, sign-in identifier, account type and optional licenses. Licensing determines which paid capabilities may be available. A license alone does not mean an employee can access every application. When employment ends, disabling sign-in prevents future authentication while session revocation tries to invalidate active refresh sessions. Some existing access tokens or applications can continue until their own lifetime or reevaluation rules apply.","technical":"Choose the authoritative source of the identity before editing a synchronized user: on-premises directory attributes may overwrite cloud-only changes. A UPN should be unique and compatible with a verified domain. Direct and group-based licensing have different lifecycle behavior. For departure, coordinate application deprovisioning, group removal, token/session revocation, device recovery and retention obligations. Microsoft's real token/session behavior is richer than this simulator's revoked flag.","visual":[["HR event","Employee joins or leaves organization"],["User identity","Account, UPN, attributes and source of authority"],["License","Available Microsoft cloud service entitlements"],["Resource access","Group memberships and enterprise application assignments"],["Offboarding","Disable, revoke sessions, deprovision and audit"]],"caseStudy":"An analyst joins Finance, receives a license and access to Finance Reports. Two weeks later the analyst moves departments. Avoid allowing old Finance access to linger.","steps":[["Create a new training user","portal/users","Use a unique fictional email ending in .test and a distinct name.","A new identity must not collide with an existing UPN."],["Assign an available simulated service license","portal/users","Open the created user record and assign a training-only license.","Availability, entitlement and cost must be checked separately in a real tenant."],["Check application assignments","portal/apps","Confirm there is no automatic entitlement merely because the account exists.","Authentication and application authorization are separate decisions."],["Practice controlled session revocation","portal/users","Use the user detail screen and observe the local session-revoked indicator.","In a real tenant distinguish refresh-session revocation, access-token validity, and account disablement."]],"symptom":"The employee signs in but cannot open Finance Reports after license assignment.","reason":"The app may require explicit user/group assignment independent of licensing.","recover":"Inspect the user’s group memberships, enterprise app assignment and related sign-in outcome. Grant only the required entitlement.","q":"A user was issued Microsoft 365 E5 but sees 'Access not assigned' for Finance Reports. What should you check?","choices":["The enterprise application's user or group assignment","The employee's desktop wallpaper","Make the user Global Administrator","Disable Conditional Access for all staff"],"answer":0,"feedback":"Licensing does not automatically grant every app entitlement. Inspect the application's assigned users and groups.","evidence":"Provide a screenshot-free fictional record showing user UPN, type, license, app assignment, and an offboarding checklist.","lab":"user","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/how-to-create-delete-users"},{"id":"group-app-access","domain":"User identities","title":"Groups, assignments, and effective application access","group":"Create, configure, and manage Microsoft Entra identities","minutes":40,"skillHint":"groups, app assignment, effective access","objective":"Give a normal user app access through a security group and verify that other users do not receive the same entitlement accidentally.","analogy":"The employee has a badge (identity); the Finance group is the permissions roster; the Finance Reports app checks that roster before opening the door.","beginner":"An employee's ability to log into Entra is not the same as their permission to use a business application. A security group can collect people who share an entitlement. Enterprise application assignments can reference users or groups; this simplifies onboarding, but it also means a wrong group member can receive unexpected access. A tenant administrator role is not needed simply to open Finance Reports.","technical":"Effective application assignment depends on the target application's assignment settings and how user/group access is processed. Group membership, nested-group semantics and group-based application assignment support vary by app and Microsoft configuration. Distinguish enterprise app assignment from application-specific roles and authorization inside the application itself. Changes may require a new sign-in or propagation before they affect existing sessions.","visual":[["Member account","Human identity signs in"],["Group membership","Explicit authorization roster"],["Enterprise application","Local service principal and assignment rules"],["Authorization","Target app maps assignment to resource access"],["Review","Remove stale group membership and confirm effect"]],"caseStudy":"A Finance analyst should see Finance Reports. The contractor working on Vendor Portal must never gain Finance Reports through a broad group assignment.","steps":[["Create a security group","portal/groups","Use a specific name such as Finance-Restricted.","Narrow group names and ownership reduce accidental grants."],["Create or inspect a normal employee","portal/users","Use a Member identity, not a tenant-wide admin role.","A normal employee should receive app access without directory administration."],["Add the employee to the group","portal/groups","Use Add group member and inspect the membership list.","The expected principal must be present before group-based assignment takes effect."],["Assign the security group to Finance Reports","portal/apps","Select Finance Reports and the Finance-Restricted group.","The group's entitlement should not be shared with unrelated apps."]],"symptom":"A contractor can open Finance Reports unexpectedly.","reason":"The contractor belongs to a broad group assigned to Finance Reports, or was assigned directly.","recover":"Inspect effective memberships and direct app assignees; remove the unintended permission and test a fresh sign-in.","q":"Where should you first look when a user is authenticated but the enterprise app says the user is not assigned?","choices":["Enterprise app assignments and the user's relevant group memberships","The user's profile photograph","The company's domain registrar invoice","A PIM role eligible for Global Administrator"],"answer":0,"feedback":"Application assignments determine app entry; unrelated directory roles or branding are not substitutes.","evidence":"Show a before/after matrix for normal employee, Finance group and Finance Reports, including a negative test for an unauthorized guest.","lab":"group-app","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/enterprise-apps/assign-user-or-group-access-portal"},{"id":"external-b2b","domain":"User identities","title":"B2B guests, cross-tenant access, and contractor boundaries","group":"Implement and manage identities for external users and tenants","minutes":45,"skillHint":"external collaboration, guest accounts, cross tenant policies","objective":"Invite an external guest and authorize exactly one application while preserving an auditable expiration and offboarding strategy.","analogy":"A vendor receives a temporary visitor pass for the project room, not the master building key. The vendor may authenticate with their own company identity, but your company controls which rooms they can enter.","beginner":"A guest's identity originates outside the host organization. Microsoft Entra can create a guest object representing that collaborator in the resource tenant. Authentication may use the guest's home identity provider. Local access remains under the host tenant's control, with cross-tenant settings and application assignments determining what is permitted.","technical":"External collaboration settings govern invitations; cross-tenant access settings control inbound and outbound B2B collaboration and trust options. Cross-tenant synchronization, B2B direct connect and guest invitation are not interchangeable. Consider tenant restrictions, trust in multifactor/device claims, sponsor ownership, access-package expiration, app-specific licensing and cross-tenant logs. The simulation models only the guest object and simple app/group assignment.","visual":[["Home tenant","Partner authenticates using their own identity"],["Cross-tenant trust","Inbound restrictions and acceptable claims"],["Guest object","Representation in the resource tenant"],["Scoped entitlement","Vendor Portal app group, not Finance Reports"],["Lifecycle","Approval, review, expiration and revoke"]],"caseStudy":"A partner analyst needs a six-week Vendor Portal project. They must not administer Entra or access the Finance application's confidential data.","steps":[["Review external-identity configuration","portal/external","Inspect the simulated collaboration and trust screen.","Guest invitations and cross-tenant settings are distinct controls."],["Invite a fictional Guest","portal/users","Use a training-only partner identity such as guest@partner.example.","Do not use your own real partner's contact information for a test."],["Create Vendor Project group","portal/groups","Add the guest only to that group.","Scoped group membership supports least privilege."],["Create Vendor Portal access and verify exclusion from Finance Reports","portal/apps","Assign Vendor Portal and inspect Finance Reports separately.","A guest object without app entitlement is not equivalent to guest authorization."]],"symptom":"An invited guest appears in the directory but cannot use Vendor Portal.","reason":"Redemption/cross-tenant restrictions or missing local app assignment, not necessarily incorrect guest credentials.","recover":"Review invitation state, cross-tenant rules, guest group, app assignment and sign-in logs. Revoke only the relevant access.","q":"Why is inviting a guest insufficient to guarantee that the guest can open the project application?","choices":["The resource tenant still evaluates app assignment and cross-tenant access controls","Every guest automatically receives all business apps","The guest needs Global Administrator","The app must be publicly accessible"],"answer":0,"feedback":"External authentication and local authorization are separate. Guests should receive only explicitly approved resources.","evidence":"Create a guest entitlement matrix showing intended portal access, prohibited Finance access, approver, expiry and revocation plan.","lab":"guest","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/external-id/external-identities-overview"},{"id":"hybrid-sync","domain":"User identities","title":"Hybrid AD identities, Connect Sync, and Cloud Sync","group":"Implement and manage hybrid identity","minutes":50,"skillHint":"connect sync, cloud sync, authentication methods, health","objective":"Identify the on-premises source of identity, select a synchronization approach, and diagnose a duplicate or conflicting cloud account safely.","analogy":"HR keeps the original employee roster. The cloud receives synchronized copies. The sync engine must know which cloud account corresponds to which employee; if the matching rule is wrong, it may duplicate or attach the wrong record.","beginner":"Many enterprises still have on-premises Active Directory Domain Services. Entra Connect Sync and Cloud Sync can provision selected directory identities into Microsoft Entra, but use different agent and configuration models. Synchronization moves identity attributes; it is not by itself the mechanism that authorizes users to apps or guarantees sign-in.","technical":"Connect Sync typically uses a synchronization server and connector configuration; Cloud Sync uses lightweight agents with cloud-managed provisioning configuration. Compare supported object types, writeback requirements, multi-forest scenarios, scale, agent availability and staging. Authentication choices include password hash synchronization, pass-through authentication, and federation. Consider immutable identifiers, source anchors, UPN matching, attribute filtering, sign-in method migration, Connect Health monitoring and rollback.","visual":[["AD DS","On-premises authoritative directory objects"],["Sync configuration","Filters, source anchors and matching rules"],["Provisioning agents","Connect Sync server or Cloud Sync agents"],["Entra users","Cloud object creation or update"],["Authentication","PHS, PTA or federation with separate sign-in health"]],"caseStudy":"The company sees an existing user's cloud account duplicated after a synchronization change. Users lose correct group assignments and cannot open their app.","steps":[["Open Hybrid identity monitoring","portal/hybrid","Inspect the seeded sync conflict and the stated mismatch.","The relevant evidence comes from source anchors and attribute matching."],["Write down the on-prem source of authority","portal/hybrid","Identify which directory attribute should own the object's value.","Cloud edits may be overwritten for synchronized attributes."],["Run the simulated repair","portal/hybrid","Use the conflict repair action after explaining the cause.","Do not delete cloud users before confirming the correct match."],["Verify sign-in separately","portal/logs","Look at a fictional sign-in event after the sync action.","Provisioning health and authentication success are independent signals."]],"symptom":"Sync job appears successful, but a user cannot sign in.","reason":"Successful provisioning does not prove the configured authentication method, credentials, Conditional Access, or app assignment will succeed.","recover":"Correlate sync health, cloud object state, sign-in logs and authentication method. Use a staged rollback rather than unplanned federation changes.","q":"Which component checks an on-premises user's password through local authentication agents?","choices":["Pass-through authentication","Microsoft Purview DLP","Application Proxy alone","Access packages"],"answer":0,"feedback":"Pass-through authentication validates through on-premises AD agents. Cloud Sync is a provisioning mechanism.","evidence":"Diagram both Connect Sync and Cloud Sync. Explain which topology you would choose and what you would monitor during migration.","lab":"hybrid","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/hybrid/sync-tools"},{"id":"passwordless-recovery","domain":"Authentication and access","title":"Passkeys, Windows Hello, TAP, and safe account recovery","group":"Plan, implement, and manage Microsoft Entra user authentication","minutes":45,"skillHint":"passwordless, Windows Hello, MFA, TAP, authentication methods","objective":"Explain why passkeys resist phishing, enroll a training identity conceptually, and design a secure lost-device recovery workflow.","analogy":"A password is like a secret phrase that can be tricked out of you. A passkey is more like a lock that proves you hold a private key without handing that private key to the website.","beginner":"Microsoft Entra supports multiple authentication methods: passwords, Microsoft Authenticator, FIDO2 passkeys, Windows Hello for Business and certificate methods, subject to policy and license. Multi-factor authentication means using qualifying independent factors. Some methods are more phishing resistant than others. A Temporary Access Pass can be used to bootstrap or recover other methods, but it must not become an uncontrolled long-term alternative.","technical":"FIDO2 uses asymmetric cryptography and origin-bound challenge responses. Windows Hello for Business binds credentials to supported devices and user gestures; it is not simply a camera password. Authentication method policy controls who may register; authentication strengths can restrict which methods satisfy a Conditional Access grant. A TAP is a time-limited admin-issued credential with security-sensitive identity proofing, scope, lifespan, revocation and audit requirements. The simulator cannot implement real cryptographic challenge/response.","visual":[["Identity proof","Approved verification before any recovery"],["TAP","Short-lived credential for account bootstrap"],["FIDO2 registration","Public/private key pair bound to legitimate service"],["Conditional Access","Require an acceptable authentication strength"],["Audit recovery","Remove temporary method and check sign-ins"]],"caseStudy":"A Finance administrator loses their security key during travel. They need to regain access without a helpdesk employee simply disabling MFA or sending an unprotected password.","steps":[["Review Authentication methods","portal/methods","Inspect which training methods are enabled.","Registration scope must be appropriate before users can add credentials."],["Issue a simulated Temporary Access Pass","portal/users","Choose a normal test user, not the emergency account.","Recovery begins only after trustworthy identity verification."],["Enable passwordless registration for a narrow pilot","portal/methods","Limit the training policy to a designated population.","A policy toggle by itself does not register an actual hardware-bound key."],["Compare Conditional Access requirements","portal/policies","Explain how authentication strength differs from generic MFA.","Phishing-resistant requirements should not be enforced before successful enrollment."]],"symptom":"A user is forced to use a passkey but has never enrolled one.","reason":"The required authentication strength or policy was enforced before provisioning the method and recovery process.","recover":"Use an approved TAP/helpdesk recovery process in an authorized tenant; never broadly disable identity protections to bypass enrollment.","q":"Which property makes FIDO2 passkeys substantially more phishing-resistant than a relayed SMS OTP?","choices":["The authentication response is cryptographically bound to the intended service origin","Passkeys are long shared passwords","Every passkey disables Conditional Access","The server keeps a copy of the user's private key"],"answer":0,"feedback":"FIDO2 challenges are bound to the legitimate service origin; the private key is not handed to the site.","evidence":"Write a lost-device recovery playbook with identity proofing, TAP lifecycle, credential registration, final sign-in verification, and emergency escalation.","lab":"methods","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/concept-authentication-passkeys-fido2"},{"id":"ca-policy","domain":"Authentication and access","title":"Conditional Access policy construction and report-only testing","group":"Plan, implement, and manage Conditional Access","minutes":55,"skillHint":"conditional access, policies, authentication strength, risk and session controls","objective":"Build a narrow report-only MFA policy and distinguish assignments, conditions, grant controls, session controls, and policy state.","analogy":"The airport security gate checks who you are, the flight you are boarding, the security signals about your trip, and the inspections required before boarding.","beginner":"Conditional Access evaluates signals such as identity, target resource, device, location and risk, then applies configured access or session controls. A policy includes who and what is targeted, which conditions are relevant, and which controls are enforced. Report-only mode lets administrators observe potential impact before activating the rule.","technical":"Multiple enabled policies can apply to one sign-in; their grant controls must be evaluated together, and a matching block can deny access. Authentication strengths and session controls differ from basic Require MFA. Include a dedicated emergency-access plan, approved exclusions and safe rollout metrics. Real policy targeting, service principal support and risk capabilities have license constraints. What If is a planning tool; review actual sign-in logs for production validation.","visual":[["Assignments","Included users/groups and targeted apps"],["Conditions","Risk, device, location and client signals"],["Grant","MFA, authentication strength or device requirement"],["State","Off, Report-only or On"],["Evidence","What If and observed sign-in logs"]],"caseStudy":"Finance wants MFA for a new reporting application without risking a tenant-wide outage.","steps":[["Create a Finance Pilot group and assign a test user","portal/groups","Use a small explicit pilot membership.","Broad targeting before observation can cause lockouts."],["Create Finance MFA Pilot in Report-only mode","portal/policies","Choose Finance Reports and Require MFA.","Report-only analyzes impact without enforcing this particular policy."],["Run What If for a pilot user","portal/whatif","Compare in-scope versus out-of-scope users.","The same policy may apply to only a subset of sign-ins."],["Inspect the sign-in logs","portal/logs","Find policy decisions and evidence in the training records.","What If predictions and actual telemetry are separate sources."]],"symptom":"MFA is not prompted after a policy was created.","reason":"It may still be in Report-only or target the wrong group/app; another enforced policy may change the effective decision.","recover":"Verify assignments, app scope, mode, user membership, enabled policy list and sign-in evidence before considering enforcement.","q":"Why should a new Conditional Access policy normally be piloted in Report-only mode?","choices":["It predicts impact without enforcing that policy immediately","It guarantees the policy is never logged","It bypasses other enabled policies","It makes all users Global Administrator"],"answer":0,"feedback":"Report-only provides observation. It does not override other active policies.","evidence":"Document policy assignment, grant, state, pilot test results, emergency access and rollback. Use the independent Finance case lab.","lab":"policy","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/conditional-access/overview"},{"id":"risk-and-logs","domain":"Authentication and access","title":"Risky sign-ins and evidence-led troubleshooting","group":"Plan, implement, and manage Conditional Access","minutes":45,"skillHint":"identity protection, risky sign-in, audit, monitor and report","objective":"Investigate a risky sign-in, distinguish user risk from sign-in risk, and choose a proportionate response with recorded evidence.","analogy":"A security camera alarm is a signal to investigate, not automatic proof of a robbery. You inspect time, location, badge and activity before deciding which doors to lock.","beginner":"Microsoft Entra records sign-ins and directory changes. A sign-in log documents authentication outcomes; audit logs show configuration changes; provisioning logs show account changes sent to connected apps. Risky sign-ins suggest a particular event may be suspicious, while user risk refers to the overall chance the account is compromised. The action should be based on evidence and security policy.","technical":"Risk-based policy settings and certain automated remediations depend on Microsoft Entra ID P2. Correlate sign-in event ID, time, IP, app, device, token context and Conditional Access detail. Look for impossible travel patterns, unusual client types, unfamiliar devices or leaked credential signals. Choose containment, user verification, token/session revocation and root-cause remediation according to the incident response runbook. KQL works only if the relevant logs have been exported and ingested into a target data store.","visual":[["Event","Successful or failed sign-in"],["Signals","Risk score, device, client, IP, app"],["Policy result","Grant, challenge or block by applicable policies"],["Investigation","Correlate with audit and provisioning evidence"],["Recovery","Contain, remediate, verify, document"]],"caseStudy":"A successful unusual-location sign-in occurs for a Finance analyst followed by a privileged app consent request. You must decide whether to revoke sessions and investigate further.","steps":[["Inspect the fictional risky sign-in","portal/risks","Read location, risk level and affected app.","Risk is a signal; verify the context before a conclusion."],["Correlate sign-in logs","portal/logs","Find the affected user and relevant sign-in outcome.","Separate successful authentication from later authorization."],["Review app consent or audit evidence","portal/defender","Investigate whether an OAuth grant expanded access.","Compromised sessions can lead to app permissions abuse."],["Record the containment decision","portal/risks","Triage the risk and document follow-up verification.","Resolving the alert is not the same as remediating root cause."]],"symptom":"A high-risk sign-in was marked 'resolved' but the attacker’s OAuth app still has access.","reason":"Identity sign-in triage did not revoke the application's separate consent or assess persistent permissions.","recover":"Review enterprise app grants, related audit changes and token/session state. Remove unneeded consent and validate that access is denied.","q":"Which evidence is most useful when deciding whether a failed sign-in was blocked by Conditional Access?","choices":["The sign-in record and its Conditional Access policy details","The user's job title alone","The tenant logo","Only an unrelated storage account metric"],"answer":0,"feedback":"The sign-in record shows policy application and results; related logs may be needed for causality.","evidence":"Write an incident record with hypothesis, matching events, containment, false-positive check, validation and follow-up.","lab":"risk","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-protection/overview-identity-protection"},{"id":"app-federation","domain":"Workload identities","title":"SAML, OIDC, OAuth and the enterprise application","group":"Plan and implement application authentication and authorization","minutes":50,"skillHint":"enterprise apps, SAML, OAuth, OIDC, SSO","objective":"Choose the appropriate protocol for a business app, distinguish sign-in from API authorization, and configure a tenant-local enterprise application assignment.","analogy":"SAML and OpenID Connect can carry an organization's proof that a user signed in; OAuth is the delegated access permit to a resource; SCIM is the provisioning service that creates or removes an account.","beginner":"Single sign-on (SSO) allows a user to authenticate through a central identity provider rather than maintaining separate passwords at each compatible app. SAML uses XML assertions; OpenID Connect layers identity on OAuth 2.0 token flows; OAuth itself is an authorization framework for resource access. An enterprise application is the tenant representation of a service principal.","technical":"SAML enterprise integration includes issuer, assertion consumer service URL, signing keys/certificates and claims. OIDC app integration uses authorization endpoints, redirect URIs, scopes, ID token validation, nonce and audience. OAuth delegated access differs from application permissions. Secret rotation and signing certificate expiry can interrupt authentication even when a user account is healthy. An app registration defines application identity/configuration, while the enterprise app governs local assignment, SSO integration and policies in the consuming tenant.","visual":[["Browser","User begins access to an application"],["Identity provider","Entra authenticates and evaluates policies"],["Protocol","SAML assertion or OIDC tokens"],["Service principal","Tenant-local app identity and assignment"],["Application","Validates token or assertion, then authorizes requested action"]],"caseStudy":"The HR vendor supports SAML SSO while a custom web app uses OpenID Connect to authenticate staff and OAuth for Graph access.","steps":[["Inspect Enterprise applications","portal/apps","Compare SSO methods for a training application.","The application’s compatibility drives protocol selection."],["Create or inspect an enterprise app","portal/apps","Open a fictional application and choose a supported sign-on method.","The simulated SSO selector does not implement real SAML assertions."],["Review app registrations","portal/registrations","Compare identity setup to the enterprise app entry.","An app registration and its service principal serve distinct purposes."],["Explore token flow","portal/flows","Identify user, client, identity provider, scope and resource audience.","An ID token should not be used as a substitute for an API access token."]],"symptom":"SSO redirects successfully but the app rejects the token.","reason":"Redirect URI, issuer, audience, token validation, certificate, clock skew or required claims may be mismatched.","recover":"Verify exact integration metadata and logs, not user admin privileges. Repair redirect/certificate/claim mapping and retest.","q":"Which protocol adds a user sign-in identity layer to OAuth 2.0?","choices":["OpenID Connect","SCIM","KQL","Azure Policy"],"answer":0,"feedback":"OIDC adds authentication semantics on top of OAuth, while SCIM is primarily provisioning.","evidence":"Create a side-by-side flow diagram for SAML, OAuth authorization code and OIDC; label which tokens are used for what.","lab":"apps","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/v2-protocols-oidc"},{"id":"app-consent","domain":"Workload identities","title":"App registrations, Graph permissions and administrative consent","group":"Plan and implement application authentication and authorization","minutes":50,"skillHint":"app registrations, service principals, delegated vs app-only, consent","objective":"Register a fictional API client, choose least-privilege delegated or application permissions, and diagnose a consent or redirect problem.","analogy":"The app registration is a blueprint that names a software client; the service principal is the instance admitted into your building; consent defines what the app may do in that building.","beginner":"Application registrations let an organization configure software authentication and requested API permissions. In delegated access, a signed-in user's permissions also matter. In application-only access, software acts without a human user and may have powerful access over many records. Administrator consent approves certain permission grants and must not be granted just because an app appears in the portal.","technical":"An app registration can contain redirect URIs, credential/certificate metadata, app roles and requested API permissions. A service principal is the local tenant object representing app identity and granted permissions. Delegated scopes use a user context; application permissions require app-only authorization and often elevated consent. Review least-privilege Graph scopes, publisher verification, OAuth grant type, client-secret expiry, certificate or federated credential design, and service principal assignment. In production, avoid exposing secrets and use managed identities where supported.","visual":[["App registration","Client ID, redirects, credentials and permissions"],["Service principal","Local tenant representation of the app"],["Consent","Approval of delegated or app-only permissions"],["Access token","Issuer, audience, scopes or roles"],["API enforcement","Resource checks token and actual granted permissions"]],"caseStudy":"A reporting daemon wants Files.ReadWrite.All application permissions for every employee's files. It might work but violates a least-privilege requirement.","steps":[["Create a fictional app registration","portal/registrations","Choose a descriptive training application name.","Differentiate software identity from a human user."],["Compare delegated and application permissions","portal/registrations","Review the available sample API permissions.","App-only grants often have a much larger blast radius."],["Record consent only after review","portal/registrations","Explicitly document who owns the app and what data is needed.","Permission review precedes tenant-wide consent."],["Inspect enterprise application assignments","portal/apps","Confirm the app's local service principal is understood.","Consent does not automatically solve every app assignment or data authorization rule."]],"symptom":"The app returns a 403 even though its registration exists.","reason":"Requested permission was not granted/consented, the token lacks appropriate roles/scopes, or the API enforces additional authorization.","recover":"Examine token claims without exposing secrets, review app and service principal grants, consent and API authorization. Grant only the minimal permission.","q":"A scheduled daemon accesses Graph with no signed-in user. What permission model is generally relevant?","choices":["Application permissions with carefully reviewed admin consent","Delegated user consent requiring an interactive user","A guest invitation as its only token","Windows Hello for Business enrollment"],"answer":0,"feedback":"A client-credentials workload acts without delegated user context; application permissions require especially careful governance.","evidence":"Write a permission justification comparing User.Read delegated to broad app-only scopes, with app owner and credential rotation plan.","lab":"registration","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/permissions-consent-overview"},{"id":"managed-workload","domain":"Workload identities","title":"Managed identities, credentials and resource RBAC","group":"Plan, implement, and manage workload identities","minutes":45,"skillHint":"managed identity, workload identity, credential management","objective":"Explain why a managed identity avoids embedded passwords and configure a narrowly scoped fictional resource grant.","analogy":"Instead of storing a permanent master key inside a robot, the organization recognizes the robot's identity and issues temporary tokens that each resource checks against specific permissions.","beginner":"A managed identity can represent a supported Azure-hosted workload and obtain access tokens without storing a client secret in application code. The identity is authenticated, but the resource still checks whether the identity has the required authorization. A token is not a blanket permission slip.","technical":"A system-assigned managed identity is tied to the lifecycle of the Azure resource; a user-assigned managed identity is a standalone identity resource reusable by supported workloads. Both are represented by service principals. Depending on the target, authorize with Azure RBAC or an appropriate resource-specific mechanism, choosing the narrowest scope. Workload identity federation can be appropriate for external CI/CD identities. Managed identities are not a substitute for all app registrations or non-Azure workloads.","visual":[["Cloud workload","Service hosted on a supported resource"],["Managed identity","Service principal representing workload"],["Token endpoint","Issue an audience-specific access token"],["Resource RBAC","Authorize only intended actions"],["Audit","Identify workload and action in logs"]],"caseStudy":"A payroll app must read a secret in PayrollVault. A junior engineer proposes Owner at Subscription because it would avoid permission errors.","steps":[["Create Payroll Worker managed identity","portal/managed","Use a fictional named identity.","A distinct workload principal enables audit and lifecycle control."],["Review available resource grants","portal/managed","Compare Secrets User to Owner at Subscription.","Authentication and resource authorization are separate."],["Grant the narrowest matching role","portal/managed","Select Secrets User on PayrollVault.","Subscription Owner is excessive for reading a vault secret."],["Open the token flow explorer","portal/flows","Explain token issuance versus the resource's authorization check.","The simulator models concepts rather than contacting Azure Managed Identity endpoints."]],"symptom":"The workload obtains a token but receives 403 when reading PayrollVault.","reason":"The required data/resource role is missing or incorrectly scoped, despite successful authentication.","recover":"Inspect resource role assignment and target scope; do not add Owner unless the application truly requires it, which this scenario does not.","q":"A managed identity has a token but Azure Key Vault denies a secret read. What is the best next step?","choices":["Inspect the resource-scoped authorization or RBAC assignment","Issue an employee TAP for the daemon","Disable logging","Make all employees subscription owners"],"answer":0,"feedback":"Managed identity token issuance authenticates the workload; the resource still enforces access rights.","evidence":"Draw an access-token flow and list which scope grants just one required secret operation.","lab":"managed","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/overview"},{"id":"governance-package","domain":"Identity governance","title":"Access packages, approvals, expiry and periodic reviews","group":"Plan and implement identity governance","minutes":45,"skillHint":"entitlement management, access packages, access reviews","objective":"Design an approved, expiring entitlement for an external contractor and verify the review's decision is actually applied.","analogy":"An access package is a temporary set of keys issued by an approved supervisor with an expiration date, and a periodic review verifies those keys are still justified.","beginner":"Identity governance manages who should retain access over time. An access package bundles resources such as groups or applications and defines who can request them, who approves, and how long they last. An access review lets managers or other reviewers decide whether existing assignments remain appropriate. Reviewing a grant does not always automatically mean that every external resource has been deprovisioned.","technical":"Entitlement management includes catalogs, access packages, assignment policies, approvers, expiration, requestors, connected organizations and resource roles. Reviews may target package assignments, groups, applications or roles, depending on licensing. Verify whether review decisions are automatically applied and what happens to downstream app accounts. Lifecycle workflows may provision or remove entitlements based on HR events. Licensing varies by governance feature and should be verified before real deployment.","visual":[["Catalog","Resource collection and ownership"],["Access package","Approved resources requested as one entitlement"],["Policy","Eligibility, approvals and expiry"],["Review","Manager or resource owner reevaluates need"],["Enforcement","Remove denied or expired access and audit"]],"caseStudy":"An outside consultant requests access to a project for 30 days. Security needs an approval, an expiry, and evidence that the contractor no longer has access after the engagement.","steps":[["Create a limited access package","portal/packages","Specify Contractor Access, Required approval and a 30-day expiry.","Approval and expiry are concrete controls, not merely notes."],["Create the target security group","portal/groups","Create Finance-Restricted or a suitable limited training group.","The entitlement must point to actual managed resources."],["Create a review against the group","portal/reviews","Choose an accountable reviewer.","A review should re-evaluate entitlement rather than rubber-stamp access."],["Inspect the access and audit result","portal/apps","Identify how revocation would remove target app access.","The simulator doesn't fully automate expiry and downstream SaaS deprovisioning."]],"symptom":"A denied contractor review is recorded, but the contractor can still open the app.","reason":"The review decision may not have been applied to the actual group/app assignment or external app provisioner.","recover":"Verify decision application, group membership, app assignment and provisioning logs; remove stale entitlements and document actual access loss.","q":"What provides a governed request process with approval and expiration for a bundle of resources?","choices":["An entitlement-management access package assignment policy","An Entra company logo","A Conditional Access report-only label alone","A random user's password"],"answer":0,"feedback":"Access package policies define eligibility, approval and assignment duration for bundled resources.","evidence":"Design an access package policy table with resources, requester, approver, duration, reviewer and revocation validation.","lab":"governance","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-governance/entitlement-management-overview"},{"id":"pim-operations","domain":"Identity governance","title":"Privileged Identity Management and just-in-time access","group":"Plan and implement identity governance","minutes":45,"skillHint":"PIM, eligible roles, privileged activation","objective":"Explain eligible versus active privileged roles and demonstrate a time-limited simulated PIM activation with audit evidence.","analogy":"The engineer has permission to request a master key from the security desk for one hour, but does not hold the master key all day.","beginner":"An always-active privileged account creates risk because a compromised session can misuse elevated access at any time. Privileged Identity Management (PIM) allows eligible assignments so a person can activate a supported role only when needed, often with approval, justification or MFA. An eligible assignment is not the same as an active administrative permission.","technical":"PIM supports time limits, approvals, role assignment visibility, activation settings and audits for supported Microsoft Entra roles, Azure resource roles and groups, with feature-specific behaviors. Compare permanent active, time-bound active and eligible assignments. Activation policies can include conditions and notifications. Emergency access is a separate safety design; do not make every recovery method dependent on a PIM activation service that may be unavailable during an incident.","visual":[["Eligible assignment","Permission to request a privileged role"],["Activation request","Justification, approval and MFA as configured"],["Active privilege","Specific role temporarily effective"],["Expiry","Role activation time window ends"],["Audit","Who requested, approved and used role"]],"caseStudy":"Jamie Cole needs Conditional Access Administrator for a one-hour security incident, not permanently. Their manager wants an activation audit.","steps":[["Inspect roles and administrators","portal/roles","Identify the narrowest appropriate privileged role.","Global Administrator is unnecessarily broad for one CA investigation."],["Create Jamie's PIM eligibility","portal/pim","Choose Jamie Cole and Conditional Access Administrator.","Eligibility allows a later controlled request; it is not active access."],["Activate the fictional eligible role","portal/pim","Perform the training activation to see the state change.","The simulator assumes a fixed one-hour window; real approval and MFA settings are richer."],["Review the audit history","portal/logs","Locate the activation event and the responsible principal.","Privilege management requires traceable evidence and eventual expiration."]],"symptom":"A user is eligible but cannot immediately edit Conditional Access.","reason":"The eligible assignment hasn't been activated or approval/verification requirements aren't satisfied.","recover":"Follow the PIM activation policy or an authorized emergency access procedure; do not permanently elevate the entire support team.","q":"What is true for a PIM-eligible role that has not been activated?","choices":["The elevated role's privileges are generally not active yet","The user has tenant-wide Global Administrator automatically","The role grants permanent application access","All access reviews are disabled"],"answer":0,"feedback":"Eligibility is a requestable right; active permissions require successful activation.","evidence":"Write a sample PIM activation record with reason, approver, start/end times, supported MFA requirement and verification log.","lab":"pim","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-governance/privileged-identity-management/pim-configure"},{"id":"scim-monitoring","domain":"Identity governance","title":"Provisioning, SCIM, sign-in logs and KQL diagnostics","group":"Plan and automate identity governance","minutes":50,"skillHint":"provisioning, SCIM, PowerShell, Graph, logs and KQL","objective":"Distinguish provisioning failures from authentication failures and design a safe telemetry-backed investigation.","analogy":"A user directory is the master roster, SCIM is the courier updating software accounts, sign-in logs are front-desk entry records, and audit logs record who changed the guest list.","beginner":"A user may exist in Microsoft Entra and sign in successfully to Microsoft 365 but still be missing from a third-party SaaS app. Provisioning moves account objects and attributes to a target system and can disable or delete target accounts on offboarding. Sign-in logs diagnose authentication attempts, audit logs track admin changes, and provisioning logs show the lifecycle delivery to apps.","technical":"Provisioning connectors, including SCIM, depend on target endpoint credentials, attribute mappings, matching rules and assignment scope. A missing account may reflect scope, matching collision, target API errors or credential expiry. Microsoft Graph and PowerShell can automate supported directory actions but should use least-privileged permissions and idempotent scripts. KQL summarizes ingested log data only; exporting sign-in logs to Log Analytics may incur ingestion and retention costs and should not be enabled without approval.","visual":[["Source identity","Authoritative user data and group scope"],["Provisioning connector","SCIM or app-specific lifecycle integration"],["Target SaaS","Create/update/deactivate account"],["Monitoring","Provisioning, sign-in and directory audit evidence"],["Recovery","Correct mapping/credential, retry and verify"]],"caseStudy":"A newly hired employee exists in Entra, but their SaaS HR account was never created. The helpdesk thinks the login password is wrong.","steps":[["Inspect enterprise applications","portal/apps","Check how users and groups are assigned to the target app.","Without the assignment/scope the user may never be provisioned."],["Review app registration and integration permissions","portal/registrations","Explain which credentials are used for SSO versus provisioning.","An SSO token and SCIM provisioning API credential solve different problems."],["Inspect sign-in and diagnostics screens","portal/logs","Distinguish user sign-in failures from provisioning errors.","Different logs answer different questions."],["Run a fictional failed-sign-in filter","portal/diagnostics","Observe a local query and describe the missing Log Analytics ingestion requirement.","The simulator does not export any live Azure telemetry."]],"symptom":"Sign-in succeeded in Entra, but the SaaS account was not created.","reason":"App provisioning or SCIM assignment/mapping failed; this is not necessarily an authentication issue.","recover":"Inspect provisioning logs, target connector credentials, mappings and scope, then retry safely and verify the target account’s state.","q":"What is the best first source for diagnosing why a SaaS account was not provisioned?","choices":["Enterprise app provisioning logs and attribute mappings","The user's webcam settings","Only the company logo","Temporary Access Pass for a workload identity"],"answer":0,"feedback":"Provisioning logs and mapping/connector configuration explain account delivery; sign-in logs alone cannot.","evidence":"Produce a fictional investigation log showing source object, assignment, mapping, connector response, target status and rollback.","lab":"logs","docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/app-provisioning/user-provisioning"}];
